        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I've finally got my hands onto this and it should resolved on latest `main`. It indeed ended up directly using tree-sitter highlighting if possible (same approach to highlighting as done in 'mini.files' and 'mini.pick' previews) and concealing helper characters (only in info window as it uses markdown).\n\nThere is also a more elaborate post processing of info lines received from LSP server which mostly concerns removing extra (usually unnecessary) whitespace.\n\n~The one downside of the current approach is that markdown code blocks appear as blank lines with tree-sitter enabled (which it is on Neovim>=0.10 as markdown parser is built-in) *and* they need to be present in order to have proper highlighting inside code blocks. This means that if info lines only contain a code block, it would have single empty line pads in top and bottom. I wouldn't say right now that it is a *huge* deal per se, but we'll see. There is a potential workaround of tweaking floating window dimensions computation to ignore top and bottom lines if they are code blocks, but this later requires a somewhat hacky `vim.api.nvim_win_call(win_id, function() vim.fn.winrestview({ topline = 2 }) end)` for every info window (to scroll past the possible first line indicating code block start).~ This is now resolved on latest `main` by adjusting window dimension computation and \"hacky\" scrolling. This became more visible after resolving #1258 in a way that shows `detail` in info window inside markdown's code block for a better highlighting. Having a single `detail` without `documentation` seems to be relatively common and having an info window be three cells high when it can be single cell high seemed to be too much.\n\n@leo-unglaub, oh, but I believe this would still not really improve signature help highlighting in PHP, because those need `<?php` first line to work properly (I *think*, as I don't really know much about PHP). As LSP server doesn't use that, there is no highlighting. As special casing certain non-Neovim-adjacent languages is a big no-no in 'mini.nvim' (*might* be possible but for *strong* cases), I don't think handling that automatically in 'mini.completion' will be possible.",
